<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agaja</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1718">1718</a>–<a href="/years/1740">1740</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as King Agaja</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Agaja was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> in the early eighteenth century who expanded his kingdom towards the coast, conquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allada">Allada</a> and Whydah. His conquests disrupted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> network managed by coastal tributaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>. In response Oyo launched repeated cavalry invasions and eventually forced Dahomey to pay tribute.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agaja.jpg" alt="Agaja" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ji-Elle · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Embl%C3%A8me_du_roi_Agadja-Mus%C3%A9e_africain_de_Lyon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
